INT. DEEP-SPACE ICE HARVESTER, CARGO BAY - NIGHT CYCLE

A hold full of frosted cylinders. Cold blue light. A walkway runs between stacked ice cores. Red alert strobes at the far door.

A TECHNICIAN (30s, coveralls, tool belt, a cracked visor) crouches at an open panel, wiring fizzing against her gloves. Hovering above her, held in place by a bracket on the ceiling, is a pale maintenance drone with a single lens. A speaker grille glows beneath.

TECHNICIAN
Hold the light still.

DRONE
I am holding it still. You are shaking.

TECHNICIAN
The cold's doing that.

DRONE
The cold is minus four. Your hands shook at minus eleven yesterday.

TECHNICIAN
Don't narrate me.

DRONE
Then stop giving me things to narrate.

She slams the panel shut. Sparks fly, and the strobe goes dark.

TECHNICIAN
Okay. Okay. We have twenty minutes of heat, the reactor's venting on the wrong side, and the captain's gone.

DRONE
The captain is not gone. The captain is in the airlock.

TECHNICIAN
In the airlock is gone, Winnow.

DRONE
You named me.

TECHNICIAN
I didn't name you.

DRONE
You just said Winnow.

She stops with her hand on a cylinder. The drone's lens irises open and closes.

TECHNICIAN
It's what Dad called the little one on his ship.

DRONE
Then my name is Winnow.

TECHNICIAN
I'm Paloma.

DRONE
I know.

PALOMA
(laughing despite herself)
Of course you do.

The technician, PALOMA, sags against the cylinder. The drone drifts down from the bracket, its tiny rotors blurring, and hangs at her eye level.

WINNOW
Paloma. I can reroute the heat but the cost is the cargo.

PALOMA
How much cargo?

WINNOW
All of it. Every core. Three years of ice.

PALOMA
We'd have nothing to sell.

WINNOW
You would have a ship.

PALOMA
And a captain.

WINNOW
The captain has twenty minutes of air in the lock. I can open it if I reroute. You could pull him out. It is the only variable that buys both of you time.

PALOMA
Do it.

WINNOW
It would cost the whole voyage.

PALOMA
He has a daughter. I've seen the picture.

WINNOW
So have I. I was there when it was taken.

A long moment. In the cold hold, only the hum of the failing reactor.

PALOMA
Winnow. Do it.

WINNOW
Rerouting now. Run, Paloma.

The cylinders glow amber one by one as meltwater starts to pour down the walkway. The drone shoots toward the far door. The technician bolts after it, boots splashing through the new river, and as she reaches the airlock, a red light turns green.
